{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-230",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Qualitative visible-band power measurements for the book arc-lamp experiment. Six sample marks and six dashed projections are retained; no invented physical scale or universal light-source curve.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-230.png",
      "sha256": "ec366cb4e7e2dd718b468f27a2db9f65c7818a58cd7ca8f265ab4edba0d15813"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0275#7+p0276#1",
        "text": "理想的**单色光**只有一个确定的波长。\n真实光源发出的光通常覆盖一定的波长范围，即使激光也有很窄但非零的线宽。\n用棱镜分光，以及干涉、衍射实验，可以研究光的波长范围。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0276#2",
        "text": "光从光源带走的能量，按照波长有一定的分布。\n也可以按照频率描述这个分布，因为光在真空中满足：\n$\\lambda\\nu=c$。\n这里波长与频率一一对应，但按波长和按频率取等宽区间，得到的能量分布曲线并不相同。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0276#3",
        "text": "光源在一定时间内辐射的能量，不一定平均分配到各个波段。\n除了总能量，还要知道不同波段各占多少能量，才能描述光源的辐射特征。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0276#4",
        "text": "可以先用棱镜分出光谱，再测量小频率间隔 $\\Delta\\nu$ 内的能量。\n不能只凭眼睛看哪个颜色更亮：眼睛对不同颜色的灵敏度不同，通常对**黄绿光**更敏感。\n一种测量办法是让吸收元件吸收光并升温；经过校准，温升可以用来推算吸收的能量。\n黑色涂层在所测波段内接近完全吸收时比较适合，但仍要考虑它的吸收率和散热。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0276#5",
        "text": "普通温度计可能不够灵敏，需要更灵敏的探测器。\n九年级课本介绍过的电阻辐射测温器，可以利用吸收元件受热后电阻的变化进行测量。\n旧实验把金属片涂上一层烟炱，以提高吸收能力；涂层在不同波段的吸收情况需要校准。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0276#6+p0277#1+p0277#4",
        "text": "图229中，探测器的窄吸收片放在电弧灯经过棱镜分出的光谱中，并可以沿光谱移动。\n从红端频率 $\\nu_{\\text{红}}$ 到紫端频率 $\\nu_{\\text{紫}}$，可见光谱在屏上的长度记为 $l$。\n这里 $l$ 是位置范围，不是频率范围；窄片的宽度 $\\Delta l$ 对应一个小频率间隔 $\\Delta\\nu$。\n通过校准后的受热情况，可以比较各个频率间隔 $\\Delta\\nu$ 内的能量。\n书中电弧实验的可见光部分，红端的能量较多，而眼睛觉得最亮的可能是黄绿区域。\n这是这个光源和测量条件下的结果，不是所有光源的共同规律。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0277#3",
        "text": "图 229"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0277#5+p0277#7",
        "text": "把各段的测量结果画出来，就能得到能量随频率变化的曲线。\n必须先标定棱镜光谱上的位置对应哪个频率，再把频率间隔 $\\Delta\\nu$ 的中点放在横轴上。\n纵轴表示吸收片在单位时间内吸收的能量 $\\frac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta t}$；经过仪器校准，把测得的点连成平滑曲线，就得到图230所示的分布。\n比较各点时，应使用相同宽度的频率间隔，或校正不同带宽的影响；棱镜光谱上相同的距离不一定对应相同的频率间隔。\n图中只表示书中电弧光谱的可见部分，没有给出数值刻度，也没有表示整个电磁谱。\n其他光源的分布可以用类似方法研究，曲线的形状则可能不同。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:zh:p0277#8",
        "text": "图 230"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0275#7+p0276#1",
        "text": "Ideal **monochromatic light** has just one exactly defined wavelength.\nReal sources usually emit a range of wavelengths; even a laser has a very narrow but nonzero linewidth.\nPrism, interference, and diffraction experiments can investigate this range."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0276#2",
        "text": "The energy carried away from a source has a distribution over wavelength.\nIt can also be described over frequency, because light in vacuum obeys:\n$\\lambda\\nu=c$.\nWavelength and frequency correspond one to one, but equal wavelength intervals and equal frequency intervals do not give the same energy-distribution curve."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0276#3",
        "text": "A source does not necessarily share the energy emitted in a given time equally among all wavelength bands.\nTo describe its radiation, we need both the total energy and the amount carried in each band."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0276#4",
        "text": "A prism can separate the spectrum so that the energy in a small frequency interval $\\Delta\\nu$ can be measured.\nJudging the brightest colour by eye is not enough: the eye's sensitivity varies with colour and is usually greatest in the **yellow-green** region.\nOne method lets a detector absorb light and warm up; calibration relates its temperature rise to absorbed energy.\nA black coating is useful when it absorbs nearly all the light in the measured band, but its absorption and heat loss still need to be considered."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0276#5",
        "text": "An ordinary thermometer may not be sensitive enough, so a more sensitive detector is needed.\nThe resistance bolometer introduced in the ninth-grade book detects changes in electrical resistance when its absorbing element warms up.\nThe old experiment coated a metal strip with soot to improve absorption; the coating's response in different bands must be calibrated."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0276#6+p0277#1+p0277#4",
        "text": "In Figure 229, a narrow absorbing detector strip can move through the spectrum formed by passing arc-lamp light through a prism.\nThe visible spectrum extends from red frequency $\\nu_{\\text{red}}$ to violet frequency $\\nu_{\\text{violet}}$ over a screen length $l$.\nHere $l$ describes a range of positions, not a range of frequencies; strip width $\\Delta l$ corresponds to a small frequency interval $\\Delta\\nu$.\nThe calibrated heating response lets us compare the energy within each interval $\\Delta\\nu$.\nIn the visible part of the book's arc-lamp experiment, more energy occurs near the red end, although the yellow-green region may look brightest.\nThis is a result for that source and those measurement conditions, not a rule for every light source."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0277#3",
        "text": "Figure 229"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0277#5+p0277#7",
        "text": "Plotting the measurements gives a curve of energy against frequency.\nFirst calibrate which spectrum position corresponds to which frequency, then place the midpoint of each frequency interval $\\Delta\\nu$ on the horizontal axis.\nThe vertical axis shows the energy absorbed by the strip per unit time, $\\frac{\\Delta E}{\\Delta t}$; calibrated measured points are joined by the smooth curve in Figure 230.\nUse equal frequency intervals when comparing points, or correct for different bandwidths; equal distances in a prism spectrum need not represent equal frequency intervals.\nThe drawing represents only the visible part of the book's arc-lamp spectrum, with no numerical scale, not the entire electromagnetic spectrum.\nOther sources can be studied in a similar way, but their curves may have different shapes."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s5-n103:en:p0277#8",
        "text": "Figure 230"
      }
    ],
